===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂ems-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|merlon}}, from {{der|en|it|merlone|t=merlon}}, from {{m|it|merlo|t=merlon}} + {{m|it|-one|pos=suffix forming {{glossary|augmentative}}s}}. ''Merlo'' is derived from {{der|en|LL.|merulus}}, {{m|la|merlus}}, possibly from {{der|en|la|merula|t=blackbird}} (as merlons resemble a row of birds perched on a wall),<ref name="OED">Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=116825|date=September 2001|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂ems-|t=black; blackbird}}. The English word is cognate with {{cog|it|mergolo|t=battlement; pinnacle}}, {{cog|pt|merlão}}, {{cog|es|merlón|t=merlon}}.<ref name="OED"/>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|architecture|military|historical}} Any of the [[upright#Adjective|upright]] [[projection]]s between the [[embrasure]]s of a [[battlement]], originally for [[archer]]s to [[shield#Verb|shield]] behind while [[shoot#Verb|shooting]] [[arrow]]s over the embrasures, or through [[loophole]]s in the merlons.
#: {{synonyms|en|{{l|en|cop|id=merlon}}|embattle|q2=heraldry}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Of the Embrasures or Merlons|section=book IV|translator=Abel Swall|title=The New Method of Fortification, as Practised by Monsieur [[w:Sébastien Le Prestre de Vauban|de Vauban]], Engineer General of France.{{nb...|With an Explication of All Terms Appertaining to that Art. Made English. With Cuts. The Second Edition. To which is Added, Exact Draughts of Dunkirk, Maestricht, Charleroy, Aeth and Mayence.}}|edition=2nd|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Abell Swall;{{nb...|and are to be sold by W. Freeman, at the Bible over-against Middle-Temple-Gate, in Fleetstreet.}}|year=1693|page=75|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ugpQIJXEnmwC&pg=RA1-PA75|oclc=1171867350|passage=The '''''Merlons''''', to the end that they may be good, ought to be made of Earth, the most eaſie to be tempered that may be: And this Earth ought alſo to be mixed with Withy Twigs, or Brambles, provided they take Root, after which they are to be lined with good Turff.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=J[ohn] Robertson|authorlink=John Robertson (mathematician)|chapter=A Treatise of Marine Fortification|title=The Elements of Navigation; Containing the Theory and Practice.{{nb...|With All the Necessary Tables. To which is Added, a Treatise of Marine Fortification. For the Use of the Royal Mathematical School at Christ’s Hospital, and the Gentlemen of the Navy.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} [[w:John Nourse|J[ohn] Nourse]],{{nb...|at the Lamb, opposite Katherine-Street, in the Strand.}}|year=1754|volume=II|section=section II (Of Batteries), paragraph 26|page=610|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=JrBEAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA610|oclc=316358261|passage=The parapet conſiſts of two parts, namely, the ''wall'' and the '''''merlons'''''. [...] The '''{{smallcaps|Merlons}}''' are detached pieces of the parapet, leaving openings called {{smallcaps|Embrasures}}, thro' which the cannon deliver their ſhot.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=|title=An Account of the Siege and Capture of Port Mahon{{nb...|from p. 258.}}|editor=Sylvanus Urban [pseudonym; {{w|Edward Cave}}]|magazine=[[w:The Gentleman's Magazine|The Gentleman’s Magazine, and Historical Chronicle]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} D[avid] Henry and R. Cave,{{nb...|at St John’s Gate}}|month=July|year=1756|volume=XXVI|section=footnote|page=319|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ek5GAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA319|column=2|oclc=192374019|passage=A battery of guns is a bank of earth thrown up to cover the men that are to ſerve the guns; this bank is cut into holes for the cannons to fire through, about 12 feet diſtant from each other. Theſe holes are called embraſures, and the maſſes of earth between them are called '''merlons'''; [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Staunton China|volume=I|chapter=Passage to the Ladrone Islands, near Macao;{{nb...|and thence to Chu-san. Transactions and Observations there.}}|page=419|passage=Along the walls, at the distance of every hundred yards, were square stone towers. In the parapets were also embrasures, and holes in '''merlons''' for archery; but there were no cannon, except a few old wrought-iron pieces near the gate.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=C[harles] W[illiam] Pasley|authorlink=Charles Pasley|chapter=Of the Space that Must be Allowed, for the Extreme Half Merlon at the Flank of a Battery, when Finished without an Empaulment|title=Rules, Chiefly Deduced from Experiment, for Conducting the Practical Operations of a Siege|location=Chatham, Kent|publisher={{...|Printed for the use of the}} [[w:Royal School of Military Engineering|Establishment for Field Instruction]]|year=1832|section=part II (Containing an Essay on the Construction of Batteries in the Field), section I (The Several Kinds of Batteries Defined.{{nb...|The Component Parts of an Elevated Gun Battery Explained in Detail.}})|page=16|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lUUIAAAAQAAJ&pg=RA1-PA16|oclc=224729515|passage=[T]he property of sloping surfaces [...] causes a cannon ball from the fortress, which enters by the mouth and strikes one cheek of an embrasure, to glance off without penetrating through the '''merlon''' on that side. Such a ball is not therefore likely to prove fatal to those men who are covered by the '''merlon''', but to those only who stand immediately behind the embrasure, and not always even to them, for if it should strike the very sloping part of the cheek, it may be reflected upwards.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Robert E[rvin] Howard|authorlink=Robert E. Howard|chapter={{w|The Devil in Iron}}|editor={{w|Farnsworth Wright}}|title=[[w:Weird Tales|Weird Tales: A Magazine of the Bizarre and Unusual]]|location=Indianapolis, Ind.|publisher=Popular Fiction Pub. Co.|month=August|year=1934|oclc=55045234|title2=The Devil in Iron|location2=[Auckland]|publisher2=The Floating Press|year2=2013|page2=42|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=scDi8j9ZLXUC&pg=PA42|isbn2=978-1-77556-213-9|passage=He had appropriated a thick tapestry rope in the great hall, and now, having reached the parapet, he looped the soft strong cord about the girl's hips and lowered her to the earth. Then, making one end fast to a '''merlon''', he slid down after her.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Barbara Brend|chapter=Lands of the West: Egypt, North Africa and Spain|title=Islamic Art|location=Cambridge, Mass.|publisher={{w|Harvard University Press}}|year=1991|page=54|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ba3LyiGfPU8C&pg=PA54|isbn=978-0-674-46865-8|passage=The exterior façades of the Cordoba mosque are carefully composed, with buttresses interspersed with doorways which reflect the framed arch of the ''mihrab''. The upper line of the wall is given a dramatic and eastern finish with a row of stepped '''merlons'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James Herbert|authorlink=James Herbert|chapter=21|title=Ash|location=London|publisher={{w|Pan Books}}|year=2012|year_published=2013|page=141|pageurl=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=jkP0iE5vBCsC&pg=PA141|isbn=978-0-230-70696-5|passage=The castle spread out before him, its high solid sandstone walls, with their crenel and '''merlon''' battlements ending in round towers, was a magnificent sight.}}

