# {{alternative spelling of|en|merlin|t=a small [[falcon]], ''[[Falco columbarius]]''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=N. P. Toll|chapter=[Fibulae.] Bow Fibulae.|mainauthor=Teresa G. Frisch; N. P. Toll|editors=[[w:Michael Rostovtzeff|M[ikhail] I[vanovich] Rostoftzeff]], A. R. Bellinger, F. E. Brown, N. P. Toll, and C. B. Welles|title=The Excavations at Dura-Europos: Conducted by Yale University and the French Academy of Inscriptions and Letters: Final Report IV: Part IV. The Bronze Objects: Fascicle 1. Pierced Bronzes, Enameled Bronzes, and Fibulae|location=New Haven, Conn.|publisher={{w|Yale University Press}}; London: Geoffrey Cumberlege, {{w|Oxford University Press}}|year=1949|page=56|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=1r9tAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA56|oclc=1112973378|passage=Most of the [[fibula]]e have a triangular molding above the notch, which probably contained wound wire. The crossbar is decorated either with a flat knob or with a Persian '''merlon'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas S. Henricks|authorlink=Thomas S. Henricks|chapter=Sport in Feudal England|title=Disputed Pleasures: Sport and Society in Preindustrial England|series=Contributions to the Study of World History|seriesvolume=28|location=Westport, Conn.|publisher=Greenwood Press, {{w|Greenwood Publishing Group}}|year=1991|page=23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=91xjXRYPyKYC&pg=PA23|isbn=978-0-313-27453-4|issn=0885-9159|passage=Smaller and less powerful falcons included the '''merlon''', the hobby, and the kesterel.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas S. Henricks|authorlink=Thomas S. Henricks|chapter=Sport in the Later Middle Ages|editors={{w|Eric Dunning}} and Dominic Malcolm|title=Sport|series=Critical Concepts in Sociology|location=London; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Routledge}}|year=2003|volume=II (The Development of Sport)|section=part 10 (Medieval and Other Pre-modern Sports)|page=160|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=TqyCioQFv6cC&pg=PA160|isbn=978-0-415-26294-1|passage=The connection between sporting activities and social rank is given a fanciful expression in the aforementioned ''[[w:Book of Saint Albans|The Boke of St. Albans]]''. In that work the author lists the types of birds considered appropriate for the various stations of human life: / The eagle, the vulture, and the '''merlon''' for an emperor [...]}}

